---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc410.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        {
          id: "s1",
          label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage",
        },
        {
          id: "s2",
          label: "Scope & Applicability",
        },
        {
          id: "s3",
          label: "ARO Recognition — a Fair Value Approach",
        },
        {
          id: "s4",
          label: "The Corresponding Asset Retirement Cost",
        },
        {
          id: "s5",
          label: "Subsequent Measurement — Accretion Expense",
        },
        {
          id: "s6",
          label: "The Credit-Adjusted Risk-Free Rate",
        },
        {
          id: "s7",
          label: "Changes in Estimated Cash Flows — the Layered Approach",
        },
        {
          id: "s8",
          label: "Environmental Obligations (410-30)",
        },
        {
          id: "s9",
          label: "Worked Example — Initial ARO Recognition",
        },
        {
          id: "s10",
          label: "Worked Example — Accretion and an Upward Revision (New Layer)",
        },
        {
          id: "s11",
          label: "Worked Example — Environmental Remediation Liability",
        },
        {
          id: "s12",
          label: "Disclosure Requirements",
        },
        {
          id: "s13",
          label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates",
        },
        {
          id: "s14",
          label: "ASC 410 vs IFRIC 1/IAS 37 — Comprehensive Comparison",
        },
        {
          id: "s15",
          label: "Statement Mapping",
        },
        {
          id: "s16",
          label: "Related Topics",
        },
        {
          id: "s17",
          label: "Glossary",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">The full cost of an asset includes its eventual retirement</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">A long-lived asset's TRUE cost includes not just acquisition and installation, but also the LEGAL obligation to dismantle, remove, or restore the site once the asset is retired — a genuinely present obligation the moment the asset is installed. ASC 410 ensures this future cost is captured as part of the asset's initial cost, rather than left unrecognised until the retirement activity actually occurs.</div></div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">More detailed and prescriptive than IAS 37's general approach</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 410 provides considerably more DETAILED, PRESCRIPTIVE initial measurement guidance for asset retirement obligations than IAS 37 provides for decommissioning provisions generally — requiring FAIR VALUE (via an expected present value technique) rather than a simple "best estimate," and a distinctive CREDIT-ADJUSTED discount rate mechanic with LAYERED remeasurement (Sections 3, 6, 7) that has no precise IFRS equivalent.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Legal retirement obligations and environmental remediation</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 410-20 applies to LEGAL obligations associated with the RETIREMENT of tangible long-lived assets (e.g. decommissioning a nuclear facility, plugging an oil well, removing leasehold improvements at lease end). ASC 410-30 applies to ENVIRONMENTAL REMEDIATION liabilities, including obligations arising under environmental laws such as CERCLA (Superfund) in the United States.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>ARO Recognition — a Fair Value Approach</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Recognised at fair value when incurred</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">An asset retirement obligation is recognised when INCURRED — typically when the related long-lived asset is INSTALLED or ACQUIRED — PROVIDED its fair value can be REASONABLY ESTIMATED, using an EXPECTED PRESENT VALUE technique (probability-weighting different possible settlement scenarios and discounting). This FAIR-VALUE-BASED initial measurement is more prescriptive and technique-specific than IAS 37's general "best estimate" approach to decommissioning provisions, which does not mandate a specific expected-present-value probability-weighting technique in the same explicit form.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Corresponding Asset Retirement Cost</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Capitalised as part of the related asset</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">The initial ARO liability is offset by CAPITALISING an equivalent "asset retirement cost" as part of the CARRYING AMOUNT of the related long-lived asset, subsequently DEPRECIATED over the asset's useful life — the same basic combined mechanic (a decommissioning provision paired with an added asset cost) that IAS 16/IAS 37 achieve together, even though the initial MEASUREMENT technique itself differs (fair value/expected present value here, versus IAS 37's best estimate approach).</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Subsequent Measurement — Accretion Expense</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The liability grows each period through accretion</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Each period, the ARO liability INCREASES through ACCRETION EXPENSE — reflecting the passage of time as the obligation moves closer to its eventual settlement date — recognised as an OPERATING expense (not interest expense) in the income statement. This is conceptually similar to IAS 37's "unwinding of the discount," though ASC 410's DISCOUNT RATE mechanic (Section 6) works quite differently.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The Credit-Adjusted Risk-Free Rate</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely different discount rate mechanic than IAS 37</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">ASC 410 discounts the ARO liability using a <strong>CREDIT-ADJUSTED RISK-FREE RATE</strong> — a rate reflecting the entity's OWN credit standing, FIXED at the date the liability (or a given LAYER of it) is initially recognised, and NOT subsequently updated for that layer even if market interest rates or the entity's credit standing change. <strong>IAS 37, by contrast, uses a CURRENT market-based discount rate</strong>, reflecting current assessments of the time value of money and risks specific to the liability, and REMEASURES the entire liability using that current rate at each reporting date. This is a genuinely different mechanic: US GAAP "locks in" a rate per layer at inception, while IFRS continuously updates a single rate for the whole liability.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Changes in Estimated Cash Flows — the Layered Approach</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A distinctly US GAAP layering mechanic</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">When the estimated AMOUNT or TIMING of future retirement cash flows subsequently CHANGES, ASC 410 requires treating each such change as a NEW, SEPARATE LAYER of the liability: UPWARD revisions are measured using the CURRENT credit-adjusted risk-free rate applicable at the time of the revision, while DOWNWARD revisions are measured using the ORIGINAL discount rate applicable to the layer being reduced. Over time, a single ARO can therefore consist of MULTIPLE layers, each carrying its OWN historical discount rate — a distinctly layered measurement approach not present in the same form under IAS 37/IFRIC 1, which simply remeasures ONE liability at a SINGLE current discount rate each period, without separate historical layers.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Environmental Obligations (410-30)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A general threshold, with detailed US-specific regulatory guidance</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Environmental remediation liabilities are recognised when PROBABLE and REASONABLY ESTIMABLE — broadly the same general recognition threshold ASC 450 applies to loss contingencies generally, layered with SPECIFIC, DETAILED guidance addressing US environmental regulatory contexts, including allocating shared remediation costs among "potentially responsible parties" (PRPs) under CERCLA (Superfund) legislation. This detailed PRP allocation guidance has NO direct IFRS analog, given its foundation in specific US environmental law.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Initial ARO Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Capitalising a decommissioning obligation</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A drilling platform's decommissioning obligation</div><div class="wk-body">An entity installs a drilling platform with a legal obligation to dismantle and remove it in 20 years. The estimated fair value of the retirement obligation, using an expected present value technique and a credit-adjusted risk-free rate of 6%, is $1,200,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">Debit Asset retirement cost (added to platform's carrying amount) $1,200,000 / Credit Asset retirement obligation (liability) $1,200,000. The $1,200,000 asset retirement cost is DEPRECIATED over the platform's useful life alongside the platform itself.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Accretion and an Upward Revision (New Layer)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Layering a subsequent estimate increase</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Year-one accretion, then a cost estimate increase</div><div class="wk-body">Continuing the $1,200,000 ARO example at a 6% credit-adjusted rate: Year 1 accretion = $1,200,000 × 6% = $72,000, bringing the liability to $1,272,000. At the start of Year 2, a change in environmental regulation increases the estimated retirement cost, adding a NEW $300,000 layer (present value), now discounted at the CURRENT credit-adjusted risk-free rate of 7% (since the original 6% rate applied only to the original layer).</div><div class="wk-answer">Year 1: Debit Accretion expense $72,000 / Credit ARO liability $72,000 (liability now $1,272,000). Start of Year 2: Debit Asset retirement cost (additional) $300,000 / Credit ARO liability $300,000 — this NEW layer accretes going forward at 7%, while the ORIGINAL $1,272,000 layer continues accreting at its own original 6% rate — TWO separately tracked layers with different embedded rates, a distinctly US GAAP mechanic.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example — Environmental Remediation Liability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Recognising a probable, estimable remediation obligation</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Soil contamination remediation</div><div class="wk-body">An entity is identified as one of several potentially responsible parties (PRPs) for soil contamination at a former manufacturing site. Remediation is PROBABLE, and the entity's allocated share of estimated total remediation cost is reasonably estimable at $2,400,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">The entity recognises a $2,400,000 environmental remediation liability, since both the "probable" and "reasonably estimable" thresholds under ASC 410-30 are met — with the specific $2,400,000 allocation reflecting the entity's negotiated or estimated PRP cost-sharing percentage under the applicable environmental regulatory framework.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 410 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>General description of asset retirement obligations and the associated long-lived assets</span><span>Reconciliation of the beginning and ending ARO liability balance, showing accretion, new liabilities incurred, and revisions</span><span>Fair value of assets legally restricted for settling ARO liabilities</span><span>Nature and estimated range of environmental remediation liabilities recognised</span></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a legal retirement obligation exists and its fair value is reasonably estimable</span><span>Allocation methodology for shared PRP environmental remediation costs</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Timing and amount of future retirement cash flows, and the appropriate credit-adjusted risk-free rate</span><span>Total estimated environmental remediation costs and the entity's allocated share</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>ASC 410 vs IFRIC 1/IAS 37 — Comprehensive Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 410 vs IFRIC 1 and IAS 37</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 410)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRIC 1 / IAS 37)</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Initial measurement basis</span><span>FAIR VALUE via expected present value technique</span><span>BEST ESTIMATE of the expenditure required to settle the obligation</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Discount rate</span><span>Credit-adjusted RISK-FREE rate, FIXED per layer at inception</span><span>CURRENT market-based rate, REMEASURED for the whole liability each period</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Treatment of subsequent estimate changes</span><span>New LAYERS, each with its own historical discount rate</span><span>Single liability, fully remeasured at the current rate each period</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>US-specific environmental (PRP/Superfund) guidance</span><span>Extensively codified in 410-30</span><span>No equivalent — addressed under general IAS 37 provisions principles only</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">The discount rate mechanic is the most consequential practical divergence — US GAAP's layered, historical-rate approach can leave an ARO liability reflecting several different embedded interest rates simultaneously, whereas IFRS always presents a single, currently-discounted figure.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 410 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Initial ARO recognition</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Liability recognised; offsetting asset retirement cost capitalised</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Accretion expense</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised as an operating expense, increasing the liability</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Upward revision (new layer)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>New layer added at the current credit-adjusted rate</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Environmental remediation liability recognition</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised when probable and reasonably estimable</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 360</span><span>Governs the long-lived asset the capitalised asset retirement cost is added to and depreciated with</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 450</span><span>Supplies the general probable/reasonably-estimable contingency threshold this Topic's environmental guidance builds on</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 405</span><span>Joint and several liability guidance frequently applies to shared environmental remediation obligations</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary preview</div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Credit-adjusted risk-free rate</div><div class="glossary-def">A discount rate reflecting the entity's own credit standing, fixed at the date a given layer of an ARO liability is initially recognised.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Layer</div><div class="glossary-def">A distinct portion of an ARO liability, added when estimated cash flows change, each carrying its own historical discount rate.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Accretion expense</div><div class="glossary-def">The periodic increase in an ARO liability reflecting the passage of time, recognised as an operating expense.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Potentially responsible party (PRP)</div><div class="glossary-def">A party identified under US environmental law (such as CERCLA/Superfund) as potentially liable for contamination remediation costs.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.ASC410} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

